@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .
@prefix rxyz: <http://rosyc.xyz#> .


rxyz:me a schema:Person ;
    rxyz:x 800 ; rxyz:y 450 ;
    schema:givenName "Robbe" ;
    schema:familyName "Claeys" ;
    schema:birthDate "2000-04-03"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:birthPlace [
        a schema:Place ;
        schema:name "Ghent, Belgium" ;
    ] ;
    # rdfs:comment "Hahahaha this is me! I'm a person." ;
.

rxyz:MyBlog a schema:Blog ;
    rxyz:x -300; rxyz:y -150 ;
    schema:name "My Blog" ;
    schema:description "This is my blog, where I write about my thoughts, opinions and beliefs." ;
    schema:blogPost rxyz:firstBlogPost, rxyz:shaclDrivenUI ;
.

rxyz:firstBlogPost a schema:BlogPosting ;
    rxyz:x 0; rxyz:y 500 ;
    schema:author rxyz:me ;
    schema:headline "What am I looking at?" ;
    schema:datePublished "2025-01-01T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:dateModified "2025-01-01T00:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:abstract "My very first blog post: a meta article about this website" ;
    schema:keywords "Semantic web", "Node-based UIs" ;
    schema:articleBody """Welcome to my revamped personal website and knowledge graph `robsyc.xyz` !

The goal of this web application is three fold:
1. To bridge traditional semantic web technologies with contemporary web development and machine learning practices. 
2. To express my thoughts, opinions and beliefs and to share my work in a structured machine- and human-readable way.
3. To fuck around a bit; a playground for tinkering with shit that vaguely interest me.

Expect unconventional UX, radical takes, and my occasional stab at zoomer-humor. I sincerely hope you enjoy lurking through my brain!""" ; 
.

rxyz:shaclDrivenUI a schema:BlogPosting ;
    rxyz:x 450; rxyz:y 50 ;
    schema:author rxyz:me ;
    schema:headline "SHACL-Driven UI: From Shapes to Components" ;
    schema:datePublished "2025-05-15T10:30:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:dateModified "2025-05-15T10:30:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:abstract "How SHACL shapes can drive dynamic UI generation in modern web applications" ;
    schema:keywords "SHACL", "RDF", "Svelte", "Dynamic UI" ;
    schema:articleBody """## The Problem with Hardcoded Mappings

Traditional approaches to visualizing RDF data involve writing custom code for each entity type. This creates duplication: the shape definition lives in TTL, but the UI mapping lives in TypeScript.

## Enter SHACL

SHACL (Shapes Constraint Language) already describes:
- What properties an entity should have
- Their datatypes and cardinalities  
- Validation constraints

Why not use this same information to generate UI components?

## The Solution

By loading `shapes.ttl` into our RDF store and parsing the NodeShapes, we can:
1. Dynamically discover which entity types have UI representations
2. Extract property definitions for form generation
3. Validate data before submission

This is the foundation for truly **shape-driven** user interfaces.""" ;
. 